<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#parthian-shot">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parthian shot</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Parthian shot was a mounted archery tactic in which a horse archer, real or feigning retreat, turned in the saddle to shoot at pursuing enemies. It was characteristic of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire</a>'s cavalry and of other steppe peoples. The phrase survives in English, often as parting shot, for a final remark made on leaving.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parthian-shot.jpg" alt="Parthian shot" width="311" height="430" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OttomanHorseArcher.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Parthian shot was a hit-and-run tactic of light cavalry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">Horse archers</a> made a real or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreat</a> at full gallop, then turned their bodies around to shoot at the pursuing enemy, who was often slower and more heavily armoured. The manoeuvre demanded great skill in both riding and archery, since both of the rider's hands held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/composite-bow">composite bow</a> while his body faced backwards. The stirrup had not yet been invented in Parthian times, so the archer kept his seat and guided his horse by balance alone.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>, an ancient Iranian empire, used the tactic to great effect in their victory over the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-licinius-crassus">Crassus</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carrhae">Battle of Carrhae</a> in 53 BCE. After them and their successors the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>, it was characteristic of most nomads of the Eurasian Steppe, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, Turks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Magyars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koreans">Koreans</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, but it was also used by the Urartians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a>. Silius Italicus attributed a similar tactic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> it served the Hungarians at the Battle of Lechfeld in 910 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a> and Swabia, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alp-arslan">Alp Arslan</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-manzikert">Battle of Manzikert</a> in 1071 against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghurid-empire">Ghurid Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-of-ghor">Muhammad of Ghor</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-tarain">Second Battle of Tarain</a> in 1192 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> and infantry of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> confederacy. The Mongol general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subutai">Subutai</a> used it against the Polish at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-legnica">Battle of Legnica</a> in 1241, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-karnal">Battle of Karnal</a> in 1739.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The phrase is also used metaphorically to describe a barbed insult delivered as the speaker departs. Samuel Butler used the image in An Heroical Epistle of Hudibras to His Lady (1678): &quot;You wound, like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>, while you fly, And kill with a retreating eye.&quot; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-conan-doyle">Arthur Conan Doyle</a> wrote in A Study in Scarlet (1886) of a character who, with a Parthian shot, walked away and left two rivals open-mouthed behind him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Parthian shot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The latter were known for the “<b>Parthian shot</b>”, in which riders feigned retreat and fired arrows backwards from the saddle, a tactic that proved devastating against slower infantry armies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/parthian-empire">Parthian Empire · Military</a></li></ul></section>
